{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":450602,"duration_api_ms":531201,"num_turns":32,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"6da92c31-7708-4512-8997-01b0b4d085d3","total_cost_usd":2.010184,"usage":{"input_tokens":20,"cache_creation_input_tokens":96862,"cache_read_input_tokens":1250983,"output_tokens":17072,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":96862,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":20,"outputTokens":17072,"cacheReadInputTokens":1250983,"cacheCreationInputTokens":96862,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.6577790000000003,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":203,"outputTokens":13464,"cacheReadInputTokens":1326020,"cacheCreationInputTokens":121824,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3524050000000001,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"summary":"Thorough review of per-tick mass budget tracking implementation (tanksim-6e5.1.2.1). The implementation is solid and meets all acceptance criteria. Budget tracking is zero-cost when disabled (Option-gated), canonical total_nitrogen()/total_carbon() helpers are defined in one place covering all 16 N-bearing and 13 C-bearing pools, closed-system ticks enforce near-zero net delta (1e-6 mg tolerance), and tests can inspect per-tick budget deltas. All 18 budget tracking tests pass, and the full test suite has no regressions.\n\nThe mass accounting model is internally self-consistent: biomass pools track N/C via composition-specific density functions (e.g., plant_nitrogen_mg = 28 mg N/g, algae_nitrogen_mg = 35 mg N/g), and cross-pool transfers (plant→detritus, microbe→DON/DOC, shrimp→detritus) are designed so tracked N and C are conserved even though the gram-mass values change between pools with different element densities. I verified the math for all major transfer pathways (plant senescence, trim plants, clean filter, dead shrimp, water change export/import) and confirmed N/C conservation in each case.\n\nKey design choices reviewed: (1) dissolved_feed_residue_g_total is correctly excluded from budget components (it's bookkeeping that mirrors DOC/DON), (2) water change explicit budget correctly pre-computes in/out before mutation, (3) invariant enforcement is captured as a budget stage so any clamping is visible, (4) the chemistry system's atmospheric CO2 exchange is intentionally treated as an open system for carbon. Two minor amendments proposed.","amendments":[{"body":"The runtime carbon conservation guard (`tick_has_closed_system_carbon` in engine.rs:551-562) is effectively disabled on every tick where the chemistry system produces nonzero DIC flux. With default ProcessParams (zero DIC exchange rates), the guard works. But any preset configuration that enables atmospheric CO2 exchange (nonzero `respiration_dic_rate_mg_c_per_g_per_hour` or `photosynthesis_dic_rate_mg_c_per_g_per_hour`) silently disables carbon conservation enforcement for the entire tick, meaning carbon leaks in OTHER systems (plant growth, algae, shrimp, etc.) go undetected at runtime. The test suite catches such leaks because it uses zeroed rates, but production gameplay does not benefit from the guard. Consider: (a) adding a code comment documenting this as a known limitation for downstream beads (tanksim-6e5.1.2.4 tracing, tanksim-6e5.3.4 bookkeeping audit), or (b) in a future bead, tracking atmospheric exchange as an explicit BudgetEntry source/sink so the guard can subtract it from the carbon delta rather than disabling the check entirely.","rationale":"tanksim-6e5.3.4 (downstream dependent: 'Audit feeding, detritus, DOC, and mineralization bookkeeping end to end') will need to reason about carbon conservation in realistic preset configs. If the guard is silently disabled for carbon in those scenarios, the audit bead may falsely assume the guard is protecting against carbon leaks. An explicit limitation note now saves debugging later."},{"body":"The `action:trim_plants` handler (engine.rs:335-347) routes trimmed plant matter entirely to `fine_detritus_g_total` (as an internal transfer), rather than treating it as an export from the system. In typical aquarium practice, the hobbyist removes trimmed plant matter from the tank (an N/C export). The budget guard accordingly treats trim_plants as a closed-system action. If the intended gameplay model is 'player removes trimmings from the tank', trim_plants should: (1) NOT add to fine_detritus, and (2) be added to the open-flux action list in `entry_has_open_action_flux`. If the intended model is 'trimmings decompose in the tank', the current implementation is correct. This is a design clarification, not a conservation bug.","rationale":"This affects whether trimming plants is an effective nutrient-export mechanism for players (important for gameplay balance). If trimmings stay in the tank, the only nutrient export tools are water changes and siphoning detritus. The downstream bead tanksim-6e5.3.4 (bookkeeping audit) should know the intended semantics before auditing the trim pathway."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"ebe02db7-7f86-42f6-a731-5f9517da041d"}
